                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "d8349285-a21f-3978-bf0d-31d1b69841dd",
                        "entry_id": "5419740e-9f95-3765-8ca9-aa374f3a2821",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Pertaining to or characteristic of unproductive, impoverished soil."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "bafdc507-2397-481e-af0f-92468c6fa102",
                                "sense_id": "d8349285-a21f-3978-bf0d-31d1b69841dd",
                                "text": "There was no exaggeration in Marian's definition of Flintcomb-Ash farm as a starve-acre place."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "29262a19-dc63-4714-97a1-bbe973391c1f",
                                "sense_id": "d8349285-a21f-3978-bf0d-31d1b69841dd",
                                "text": "They'd grown up together, had seen a lot of life, and were mostly inseparable, through six decades of raven, starve-acre days."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f5e9b0ac-8bf8-4c42-868c-b1e1896830e1",
                                "sense_id": "d8349285-a21f-3978-bf0d-31d1b69841dd",
                                "text": "I suggest that he was instead thinking about his birthplace, where, for a while, those infants did live to inherit the earth, at least the mined-out, falling-down, starve-acre part of it that was the town of Hailey."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "95863fab-fb9d-3630-adb1-2490306eec32",
                "word": "starve-acre",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "67b6e613-5bec-38a5-9473-85181a91de67",
                        "entry_id": "95863fab-fb9d-3630-adb1-2490306eec32",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A type of crowfoot Ranunculus arvensis, known for growing on impoverished soil."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "36186243-c41c-492d-ad77-4215ee965cfd",
                                "sense_id": "67b6e613-5bec-38a5-9473-85181a91de67",
                                "text": "Some sandy soils are much overrun with the wild radish (Raphanus raphanistrum), provincially called \" rump,\" while the clays are swarmed with starve-acre (Ranunculus arvensis), and the clivers or burrs (Galium aparine), which are very troublesome to separate from the grain when winnowed."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "664afc5c-a8f3-4b53-a28f-459f7d763ca6",
                                "sense_id": "67b6e613-5bec-38a5-9473-85181a91de67",
                                "text": "It seems that a particularly tiresome form of rauunculus, well described by the nickname of “starve-acre,\" can be cxterminated with dressings of sulphate of ammonia, and that this method is equally efficacious in ridding land of the picturesque but unprofitable poppy. Where there is more starve-acre than grass, the farmer pays twice his nominal rent, and is otherwise wasting money by growing weeds on his pasture."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "38c5669f-7837-4ed2-a0af-b217b2d00744",
                                "sense_id": "67b6e613-5bec-38a5-9473-85181a91de67",
                                "text": "It is quite common for farmers to use cereal grains for seed which contain weed seeds, e.g. starve-acre (ranunculus arvensis), docks, wild oats, rye-like brome (bromus secolinus), etc."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
